\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  real jumps 3.5 pct on cenbank  \highlight22\uc2 interventions \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 202 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Dezember 2008\par 19:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO, Dec 30 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rallied more than 3 percent on Tuesday after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  twice to add liquidity, capping a tumultuous year in which the real took a pounding.\par \par \uc2 The real <BRBY> surged 3.5 percent on Tuesday to 2.333 per dollar, clawing back from a 2 percent drop on Monday after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold $530 million in dollar repurchase agreements and an unspecified amount of dollars on the spot \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 Despite Tuesday's gains, for the full year 2008 the real shed 23.8 percent of its value, weakening after five straight years of gains. In 2007, the real strengthened 20 percent, making it one of the best performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the world.\par \par \uc2 The last time the real slumped on an annual basis was 2002, when it plunged 34.7 percent in the run-up to the election of Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva as Brazil's president.\par \par \uc2 Brazilian financial markets will be closed on Wednesday and Thursday.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Todd Benson, Editing by Chizu Nomiyama)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-BRAZIL/\b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2  (URGENT)|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|E|RBN|U|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020081230e4cu0015k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
