\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Fundamentals favor stronger Brazil  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ana Nicolaci da Costa  \par \uc2 652 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Dezember 2008\par 16:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 BRASILIA, Dec 10 (Reuters) - Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has fallen nearly 40 percent since August but looks poised to rebound next year thanks to ample \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, a relatively lively economy and the potential for tougher \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 A weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a double-edged sword for \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 , making exports more competitive but also potentially fueling inflation just when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is under pressure to cut interest rates and boost growth.\par \par \uc2 After hitting a nine-year high in August, Brazil's real <BRBY> has shed nearly 37 percent as investors have jostled for a dwindling supply of dollar-denominated credit, pulled funds from Brazil to cover losses abroad and sought refuge in U.S. assets.\par \par \uc2 The real has also suffered from the bruising drop in the prices of commodities, which make up much of Brazil's exports. Hefty losses by Brazilian companies in the derivatives markets, also increased \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility. The real traded at 2.458 against the dollar on Wednesday, weakening from around 1.560 in August.\par \par \uc2 "Up to 2.50 is the fair value for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in these poor conditions of liquidity, credit and international demand," said Alexandre Lintz, chief strategist in Brazil for \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Like many emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the real is vulnerable in the near term, with few investors willing to risk capital in the midst of the worst economic uncertainty in a generation.\par \par \uc2 Already, a deepening global recession is draining the available pool of dollars for investment in Brazil.\par \par \uc2 Net U.S. dollars outflows from Brazil totaled $7.16 billion in November, its highest outflow since 1999, according to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Foreign direct investment is expected to fall to $24 billion in 2009 from $34.7 billion through October this year, economists have said.\par \par \uc2 "The risk is for a lot of pressure unless we really see a more active \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. That could at least contain some of the volatility," said Zeina Latif, economist at ING.\par \par \uc2 MORE ACTION\par \par \uc2 However, longer-term fundamentals favor a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has taken a series of measures in recent months to keep liquidity flowing, selling dollars on the spot market and carrying out \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap auctions. Unlike other commodity exporters such as Russia, which have run down reserves as their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have weakened, Brazil has spent only $6.7 billion of its reserves to stem the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Reserves of around $207.2 billion are now actually slightly higher than in September when the crisis worsened with the collapse of \uc2 Lehman Brothers\uc2 . Analysts say that is because they are mostly held in U.S. Treasuries which gained sharply in recent months.\par \par \uc2 With such ample reserves, analysts say the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  more aggressively.\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should sell a bigger percentage of the reserves than it has done," said Alexandre Ferreira, vice president of treasury at WestLB, who said the real at 2.50 already seemed cheap.\par \par \uc2 "It is letting the real weaken a bit more than it should," Ferreira said.\par \par \uc2 Economists say Brazil's resilient economic growth as well as solid public accounts will eventually allow the real to bounce back to around 2.00 or 2.30 per dollar next year.\par \par \uc2 The economy remains a standout in a world clouded by recession, and growth will top 5 percent this year. Depending on the extent of the global crisis and the impact of domestic stimulus measures, it could grow around 3 percent next year.\par \par \uc2 "There is a gap between the implementation of policies of fiscal and monetary stimulus and their impact on the economy," Lintz said.\par \par \uc2 "We see room for a good appreciation (of the real) in the second half of (2009)," Lintz added. (Editing by Raymond Colitt and Tom Hals)\par \par \uc2 BRAZIL-REAL/ (ANALYSIS)|LANGEN|ABN|C|GRO|MTL|SOF|O|OIL|M|E|RBN|D|U  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020081213e4ca000a6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
