\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  stocks up, real cuts losses on  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 496 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 November 2008\par 15:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO, Nov 28 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's stock market rose on Friday, buoyed by mining giant Vale and banking shares, while the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  slumped even though the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively to ease the volatility.\par \par \uc2 The Sao Paulo Stock Exchange's benchmark Bovespa index <.BVSP> was up 1.92 percent at 36,907.69 points, with Vale and banking shares like Bradesco, Unibanco and Itau leading the way. On Thursday, the index slipped 0.7 percent in light trading, snapping a three-session winning streak.\par \par \uc2 Brazilian shares rose despite nagging concerns about the health of the global economy and the poor outlook for corporate profits, which weighed on stock markets in Europe on Friday. U.S. shares also opened lower after the Thanksgiving holiday.\par \par \uc2 Vale , the world's leading producer and exporter of iron ore and the second-heaviest weighted stock in the Bovespa index, gained 2.44 percent to 24.79 reais, temporarily shaking off concerns about slowing global demand for metals that have punished its stock price in recent months.\par \par \uc2 Banking shares also propelled the index higher, with Bradesco advancing 1.42 percent to 24.34 reais, Unibanco climbing 2 percent to 14.84 reais and Itau up 2.31 percent at 26.99 reais.\par \par \uc2 Brazilian banking shares have become an investor favorite of late as the sector looks poised for further consolidation following Itau's proposed takeover of smaller rival Unibanco.\par \par \uc2 "Investment funds are on a buying spree to prevent the index from closing the month in the red," said Rodnei Riscali, a director Hera Investment in Sao Paulo.\par \par \uc2 Telecoms stocks also surged, with \uc2 Brasil Telecom Participacoes\uc2  soaring 13.6 percent to 18.77 reais after its larger rival Oi Participacoes said late on Thursday that it had raised enough money to complete its takeover of \uc2 Brasil Telecom\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Oi shares were up 4.2 percent at 33.95 reais.\par \par \uc2 In the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, the Brazilian real <BRBY> slumped 2.6 percent in early trading but pared some losses after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a bid to meeting surging demand for safe-haven U.S. dollars.\par \par \uc2 The real, which has shed more than a third of its value since hitting a nine-year high in early August, was off 0.7 percent at 2.295 per dollar in early afternoon trading.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold an unspecified amount of dollars from its foreign reserves in two separate auctions and also planned to offer up to $300 in dollar swaps in another auction later in the session.\par \par \uc2 Interest-rate futures <0#DIJ:> on the BM&F commodities and futures exchange were mostly lower after data released on Thursday showed that Brazil's broadest price index, the IGP-M, rose less than expected in November.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Jenifer Correa and Aluisio Alves, Writing by Todd Benson; Editing by Kenneth Barry) )\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-BRAZIL|LANGEN|ABN|E|RBN|M|D|U|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020081128e4bs00168\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
