\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil unveils more steps to contain credit crisis\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ana Nicolaci da Costa and Fernando Exman   \par \uc2 431 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 November 2008\par 17:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 BRASILIA, Brazil, Nov 6 (Reuters) - Brazil will grant companies more time to pay taxes and will provide 10 billion reais ($4.6 bln) in loans for working capital, the country's Finance Minister Guido Mantega said on Thursday, in the latest step to help the economy brave a global credit crisis.\par \par \uc2 Companies will get an extension to pay wholesale taxes on industrial products as well as on social welfare contributions.\par \par \uc2 The national development bank, BNDES, is to offer the 10 billion reais line of credit.\par \par \uc2 "It's another 10 billion reais, on top of the 90 billion reais which the (BNDES) already has to irrigate the market directly," Mantega told reporters at an event in Brasilia.\par \par \uc2 Mantega also said the government had instructed state-run bank, \uc2 Banco do Brasil\uc2  , to make available a total of 4 billion reais to automakers' financing units to increase car loans and help spur sales.\par \par \uc2 "With these resources, we're ensuring that there will be enough financing available for the automobile industry to maintain sales in November and December," Mantega said.\par \par \uc2 New automobile sales in Brazil slumped 11 percent in October from September, the national automakers' association Anfavea said earlier.\par \par \uc2 $40 BLN SPENT\par \par \uc2 Brazil's government has taken a series of measures in recent weeks aimed at easing the global liquidity crunch, such as reducing banks' mandatory reserve requirements, offering \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap auctions, and allowing bigger banks to take over loan portfolios of distressed smaller banks.\par \par \uc2 The country's economy has been growing above 5.0 percent annually.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Henrique Meirelles, speaking at the same event, said \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  had spent $40 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a bid to meet surging demand for U.S. dollars in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 Despite the massive \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's international reserves have only been reduced by $5.1 billion, he said. \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps it used do not eat into the reserves.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  held such swap auctions daily and regularly sold dollars in the spot market in recent weeks to boost liquidity as the real plunged nearly 12 percent against the dollar in October.\par \par \uc2 The government also offered bridge loans to exporters and farmers seeing demand slump.\par \par \uc2 Mantega said the worst of the financial crisis had passed and that Brazil had a solid fiscal situation and robust domestic demand.\par \par \uc2 "Our outlook is that we can continue growing around 4 percent in 2009," Mantega said.\par \par \uc2 FINANCIAL-BRAZIL/MANTEGA|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|E|RBN|RNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020081106e4b60022i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
