\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging-Mkt Debt Weaker, Stks Lower After Late Selling Wave\b0\par\par\uc2 By Claudia Assis   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 601 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Oktober 2008\par 21:31\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Emerging market debt ended weaker Monday after a slew of credit ratings downgrades in developing Europe and despite a rally for Brazilian bonds.\par \par \uc2 Emerging-market stocks, which had opened lower but eked out gains as the session progressed, also couldn't resist a sharp downturn for U.S. equities and ended the day lower.\par \par \uc2 The spread on \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 's Emerging Markets Bond Index Global Diversified was 29 basis points tighter at 872 basis points over Treasurys in late afternoon activity, for negative returns of 0.23%.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of New York\uc2 's Emerging Market American Depositary Receipt index closed down 3.9% at 147 points, while the bank's Latin America index receded 3.3% and the Asia index ended 6.7% lower.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Brazil's benchmark bond, the Global 2040, rallied to gain 3 7/16 to 104 3/16 bid, according to Reuters. Brazil's spread on \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 's EMBIGD was 72 basis points tighter at 596 basis points over Treasurys, for positive returns of 3.13%.\par \par \uc2 "The market is mildly positive for now," a debt trader in New York said. While benchmark bonds were better bid, volumes were still lower than usual, he added.\par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real strengthened Monday against the U.S. dollar as the government defended the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. The real closed at BRL2.244 per dollar, compared to a Friday close of BRL2.327. \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  three times in the exchange market.\par \par \uc2 Adding to good news for the South American giant, Brazil's largest private bank, Banco Bradesco (BBD), Monday reported rising third-quarter net profits on robust growth in its credit portfolios. The bank's total credit portfolio reached BRL197.2 billion, up 40.8% from 2007.\par \par \uc2 Neighboring Argentina, however, was underperforming the broader market, as the aftershocks of last week's announcement of a proposed nationalization of the country's private pension system continued to be felt.\par \par \uc2 Argentina's risk premium on \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 's EMBIGD was 22 basis points wider at 1,865 basis points over Treasurys. Returns were a negative 3.08%.\par \par \uc2 Several emerging Europe countries were also significantly weaker. Ratings agency \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  lowered its credit ratings on Romania, Latvia and Lithuania, and lowered its outlook for Estonia, Croatia and Poland.\par \par \uc2 Neighboring Russia, which has been warned of a potential downgrade from S&P, was feeling the pinch as well. Russia's spread on \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 's EMBIGD was seven basis points tighter at 832 basis points over Treasurys. Returns were a negative 0.41%.\par \par \uc2 Hungary's debt was stabilizing after news on the impending financial assistance package from the International Monetary Fund over the weekend. Investors awaited concrete details of the arrangement, which brought little relief for battered local Hungarian markets Monday.\par \par \uc2 Hungary's risk premium on \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 's EMBIGD was 12 basis points tighter at 499 basis points over Treasurys. Returns were a positive 0.02%.\par \par \uc2 Poland's spread was 14 basis points tighter at 299 basis points over Treasurys. Returns were a positive 0.02%.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine, also subject of an IMF bailout, was significantly underperforming the market. Ukraine would get $16.5 billion from the multilateral, a larger-than-expected loan.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine's risk premium on \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 's EMBIGD was 88 basis points wider at 2,393 basis points over Treasurys. Returns were a negative 4.94%.\par \par \uc2 -By Claudia Assis, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-4385; claudia.assis@dowjones.com [ 27-10-08 2031GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1179 1810 4071   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020081027e4ar0013s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
