\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE:Emerging Mkt Bonds Fall As Crisis Deepens; Stks Rally\b0\par\par\uc2 635 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Oktober 2008\par 23:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    (Updates with comments, fresh levels) \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 By Giada Cardoletti Litner\f28 \par \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Emerging market debt came under intense pressure again Thursday, while developing country stocks traded in the U.S. closed flat following a highly volatile session.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the governments of \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Mexico to prop up the value of their falling \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and news that the International Monetary Fund is in discussions with a number of countries about possible loan packages, helped to pare the losses.\par \par \uc2 Both the Brazilian real and the Mexican peso, which have been pounded of late, benefited from \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The peso closed in Mexico City at MXN13.3940, compared with Wednesday's close of MXN13.8550, while the real ended at BRL2.3050 to the dollar, markedly stronger than Wednesday's close at BRL2.3795.\par \par \uc2 With 10 minutes to go before the closing bell, ADRs pared losses from a session that featured several momentum reversals, as investors went into a buying frenzy.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of New York\uc2  Emerging Markets American Depositary Receipt index closed up 0.02% at 174 points after hitting a low of 159 points earlier in the day. The Latin American ADR index climbed 0.93% and the Asian ADR index gained 0.23%.\par \par \uc2 The reversal tracked the U.S. market where the Dow Jones Industrial Average, in another roller-coaster session, closed up 2%, led by a big bounce in energy companies.\par \par \uc2 The exodus from emerging markets external debt, however, found no relief, as a combination of fear and deleveraging drove spreads sharply upward.\par \par \uc2 The spread on \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 's Emerging Markets Bond Index Global Diversified increased by 31 basis pointsto 850 basis points over Treasurys, its widest this year.\par \par \uc2 "Equities underperformed external debt and credit default swaps earlier this year (following U.S. equities), so now it's somewhat of a catch-up," said Nick Chamie, head of emerging markets research for \uc2 RBC Dominion Securities\uc2  in Toronto.\par \par \uc2 "This global credit crisis is having a significant impact on global banking systems and capital flows, so this is much more than about equity valuations. It's about unwinding of a massive credit bubble that eventually raises the overall level of risk," he added.\par \par \uc2 In Central and Eastern Europe, where worries are mounting about current account deficits and large external debts in several countries, credit spreads were at all-time highs. Five-year credit-default swaps on Hungary were trading around 650 basis points, up more than 100 basis points on the day and 202 basis points on the week.\par \par \uc2 Pakistan, Ukraine, Belarus and Hungary are among the countries with which the IMF has said it's discussing potential loan packages.\par \par \uc2 The cost of insuring sovereign debt against default for five years rose across the board Thursday, regardless of the credit quality of the sovereign.\par \par \uc2 Brazil, the emerging markets' credit heavyweight, saw the cost of insuring its debt against default for five years jump 66 basis points to 610 basis points over Treasurys.\par \par \uc2 Even China, which sits on the world's largest foreign reserves cushion and is believed to be better shielded than other developing economies from the global financial crisis, saw its five-year CDS spread climb 23 basis points to 255 basis points over Treasurys, according to Markit.\par \par \uc2 "Right now fundamentals are put on the sidelines," said Roberto Sanchez-Dahl, who manages $600 million in emerging markets debt with Federated Investors. "It's all about technicals and the large liquidity needs that put pressure on prices. People are selling whatever they can, anticipating redemption and lower prices... it's like a vicious cycle."\par \par \uc2 -By Giada Cardoletti Litner, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-4290: giada.cardoletti@dowjones.com [ 23-10-08 2141GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1179 1810 1833 1836 4071 5076   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020081023e4an0014z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
