\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  real rallies on  \highlight22\uc2 interventions \highlight\uc2 , stocks slump\b0\par\par\uc2 By Todd Benson   \par \uc2 554 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Oktober 2008\par 23:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Updates to close)\par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO (Reuters) - Brazilian stocks slumped in volatile trade and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rallied after plunging to a 3-1/2-year low Wednesday, helped by a slew of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  as fears about the global financial crisis hung over the market.\par \par \uc2 The Bovespa index of the Sao Paulo Stock Exchange tumbled for the fifth straight session, ending 3.85 percent lower at 38,593.54 points after falling as much as 6.3 percent. With the losses, the Bovespa has now plunged 48 percent since hitting an all-time high of 73,920 on May 29.\par \par \uc2 Trading was extremely volatile as fears persisted that the coordinated interest rate cuts by the world's leading central banks might not be enough to prevent the global economy from slipping into a recession.\par \par \uc2 "The coordinated actions by the central banks is not going to have a convincing effect on the credit crisis," said Jose Francisco de Lima Goncalves, chief economist at \uc2 Banco Fator\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the real, also took a pounding early in the session but recovered after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  four times to flood the market with dollars.\par \par \uc2 The real closed 0.74 percent stronger at 2.294 per dollar after plunging more than 8 percent to 2.53 in spot electronic trading at the BM&F commodities and futures exchange, its weakest intraday level since April 29, 2005.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped the real snap a five-session losing streak by dipping into its international reserves for the first time in more than five years to sell dollars on the spot \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 It sold an unspecified amount of dollars in three separate auctions. Late in the session, the bank also sold $1.3 billion in dollar swap contracts, helping to nudge the real into positive territory.\par \par \uc2 "This is basically a tug-of-war between the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the market. Today the bank decided to show that it has $210 billion in reserves as firepower," said Reginaldo Galhardo, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at Treviso Corretora, a Sao Paulo brokerage.\par \par \uc2 Despite Wednesday's gains, the real has still shed a third of its value since Aug. 1, when it hit a nine-year high. A weaker real threatens to stoke inflation in Brazil, meaning the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely to join other countries in cutting rates in the coming months.\par \par \uc2 Interest-rate futures on the BM&F commodities and futures exchange were mixed, with short-term contracts edging lower and long-term contracts rising.\par \par \uc2 At the stock exchange, energy and steel sector shares slumped on fears that a global economic slowdown will reduce demand for raw materials and oil, pushing the Bovespa index sharply lower.\par \par \uc2 State-run energy company \uc2 Petrobras\uc2  tumbled 5.65 percent to 26.70 reais as world oil prices fell more than $1 a barrel on concerns about the extent of the global financial crisis and rising U.S. inventories.\par \par \uc2 In the steel sector, Usiminas sank 8.24 percent to 26.50 reais while Gerdau tumbled 8.64 percent to 14.80 reais. Mining giant Vale also fell, sliding 0.77 percent to 25.80 reais. (Additional reporting by Jenifer Correa; Editing by Diane Craft)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-BRAZIL/ (UDPATE 2)|LANGEN|FUN   \par