\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil stocks,  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  plunge in global sell-off\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ana Nicolaci da Costa   \par \uc2 581 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Oktober 2008\par 18:32\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Updates to close)\par \par \uc2 BRASILIA, Oct 6 (Reuters) - Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  posted its biggest loss in more than nine years and stocks plummeted by as much as 15 percent on Monday, forcing the stock exchange to halt trading twice as a deepening financial crisis battered markets around the globe.\par \par \uc2 The latest scare came over the weekend when moves by European governments to rescue large banks and protect ordinary depositors triggered widespread concerns about the fate of the world economy.\par \par \uc2 The Bovespa index <.BVSP> of the Sao Paulo Stock Exchange plunged near a two-year low but pared losses late in the session to end 5.43 percent lower at 42,100.79 points. The index has now fallen a whopping 43 percent from its all-time high of 73,920 reached on May 29.\par \par \uc2 "There's no way to explain this. ... All parameters have been lost," said Joao Medeiros, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Pioneer, a Sao Paulo brokerage. "Panic is the word for this."\par \par \uc2 As shares nosedived early in the session, the stock exchange was forced to halt trading twice. The last time it suspended trading more than once on the same day was in January 1999, when a financial crisis forced Brazil to allow its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to float freely against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the real <BRBY>, also took a pounding on Monday, clocking its biggest one-day loss since it was allowed to float nine years ago. The real slumped 7 percent to a two-year low of 2.20 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 With Monday's losses, the real has weakened 19.2 percent so far this year, nearly wiping out a 20 percent surge in 2007.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  during the session in a bid to quench surging demand for greenbacks, offering dollar swaps for the first time in more than two years. It sold $1.47 billion in swaps of $2.1 billion on offer in an auction.\par \par \uc2 "I think what is happening to the real in particular is the end of an era," said Marcelo Carvalho, chief Brazil economist at \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The last five years were extremely favorable for emerging countries such as Brazil; this world has ended, the global environment is one of significant slowdown."\par \par \uc2 The market turmoil also sent interest-rate futures <0#DIJ:> sharply higher on the BM&F commodities and futures exchange.\par \par \uc2 At the stock exchange, commodity stocks were among the hardest hit on fears a global financial crisis will hurt demand for metals and oil. Crude prices fell to eight-month lows below $90 a barrel while copper prices fell more than 6 percent.\par \par \uc2 State-run oil giant \uc2 Petrobras\uc2  fell 3.23 percent to 30.00 reais and mining company Vale dropped 6.8 percent to 26.99 reais. Steelmaker Usiminas plunged 11 percent to 30.50 reais.\par \par \uc2 Banks also took a thumping, with Banco Bradesco falling 6.58 percent to 25.55 reais, Banco Itau tumbling 4.74 percent to 26.55 reais and Unibanco slipping 2.63 percent to 15.89 reais.\par \par \uc2 The losses tracked those on major stock markets, with U.S. equities plunging and the key pan-European share index suffering its worst one-day percentage fall on record. (Additional reporting by Jenifer Correa in Sao Paulo, Writing by Todd Benson; Editing by Leslie Adler)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-BRAZIL/ (UPDATE 2) (PIX)|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|E|RBN|RNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020081006e4a6001hu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
