\par\par \b \uc2 Investors Bail From Brazil Assets On US Crisis Fears\b0\par\par\uc2 By Alastair Stewart   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 499 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 2008\par 19:08\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO (Dow Jones)--Investors bailed out of Brazilian assets with a passion Wednesday amid fears that the U.S. financial crisis may not be over yet.\par \par \uc2 Dollars flew out of the country as investors sought to cover liabilities and reserve requirements abroad and put money in safe-haven investments such as U.S. Treasury bonds.\par \par \uc2 "We are seeing funds and banks repositioning in safe assets amid the turmoil. Brazilian assets don't fit into that profile," said Roberto Padovani, chief strategist at WestLb bank.\par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real slumped more than 3% to trade at BRL1.885 to the U.S. dollar by early afternoon, taking the losses for the month to stunning 15%. Meanwhile, the benchmark Ibovespa index was 5.9% lower at 46,325 points. The index has shed 17% this month.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. Federal Reserve's decision to bail out insurer \uc2 American International Group Inc\uc2 . (AIG) did little to calm global markets as investors began speculating on who would be the next casualty of the current crisis.\par \par \uc2 "Investors continue to repatriate funds to cover losses suffered abroad and to reposition in rock-solid positions ahead of further revelations. It's a fire-sale scenario," said Miriam Tavares, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at the AGK brokerage in Sao Paulo.\par \par \uc2 Traders said investors were also repositioning after the U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission, or SEC, imposed a firm close-out requirement on short sellers and their brokers.\par \par \uc2 There is also a lot of money moving into derivatives on the Brazilian Mercantile and Futures Exchange, or BM&F, as local investors seek hedges. That movement is weighing on the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said AGK's Tavares.\par \par \uc2 While local officials insist that the Brazilian financial system isn't suceptible to contagion from abroad, investors are becoming increasingly concerned about the impact of the crisis on the wider economy.\par \par \uc2 The decline of commodities prices will hit Brazil's larger companies and the trade balance, while the tightness of credit on the international market may limit the expansion plans of local companies.\par \par \uc2 Earlier Wednesday, Finance Minister Guido Mantega promised the government would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if corporate credit remained tight.\par \par \uc2 The idea that \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  can decouple from the crisis has long gone out of the window. Now, investors are simply attempting to gauge the extent of the damage.\par \par \uc2 For example, the accentuated decline of the real could lead to increased local inflation pressure at a time when the economy shows signs of slowing, leaving the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  with a difficult conundrum to solve.\par \par \uc2 Official 12-month inflation stands at a perilous 6.2%, while the Selic base interest rate is currently at 13.75%.\par \par \uc2 Faced with a multitude of uncertainties, liquidity is the priority and cash is increasingly the position of choice, noted Padovani.\par \par \uc2 -By Alastair Stewart; Dow Jones Newswires; 5511 2847-4520; alastair.stewart@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (Rogerio Jelmayer contributed to this report.) [ 17-09-08 1708GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020080917e49h000wb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
