\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil Real Closes Weaker On Failed Tax Vote, Credit Mkts   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Jeff Fick   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 430 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Dezember 2007\par 19:35\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 RIO DE JANEIRO (Dow Jones)--The Brazilian real closed weaker against the U.S. dollar Thursday after the government failed to get a key tax measure passed and amid renewed concerns about global credit markets.   \par \par \uc2 The real closed at BRL1.7810 in trading on the Brazilian Mercantile & Futures Exchange, or BM&F, weaker from Wednesday's close at BRL1.7720.   \par \par \uc2 Traders said the government's failure to win an extension of the CPMF financial transactions tax, which expires Dec. 31, overhung the market Thursday. The tax generated about 40 billion Brazilian reals ($22.6 billion) in revenue for the government.   \par \par \uc2 The measure's defeat could generate serious budget difficulties in 2008, as well as cause the government to revise its fiscal targets, analysts said.   \par \par \uc2 Brazil's government has pledged to post a public sector primary budget surplus this year and next equivalent to 3.8% of gross domestic product, or more than BRL90 billion annually. The surplus funding is aimed at paying down the public sector debt, which as of October stood at BRL1.13 trillion, or the equivalent of about 43.7% of GDP.   \par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Guido Mantega said the government would maintain its commitment to meeting fiscal targets. In the meantime, the government will suspend all budget spending and planned exemptions until it announces measures to cover the CPMF tax loss next week, Mantega said.   \par \par \uc2 Global credit concerns also weighed on financial markets, analysts said. Wednesday's injection of liquidity into credit markets, while well met, also raised apprehension about future negative surprises from financial institutions exposed to the troubled U.S. subprime mortgage sector.   \par \par \uc2 The real had traded stronger throughout the session after weakening early, but \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  late in the session sapped the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's momentum.   \par \par \uc2 The bank bought U.S. dollars at the snap auction Thursday for BRL1.7800 per dollar, the bank said. The bank did not reveal the volume of dollars purchased. The bank said it is purchasing dollars to build up foreign reserves.   \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the risk to investing in Brazil as measured by the  \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2  Emerging Markets Bond Index Plus, or EMBI+, tightened to a spread of 207 basis points over U.S. Treasuries. Brazil's benchmark Global 40 bond traded 1/2 lower at a bid of 133 3/4 as of 1809 GMT.   \par \par \uc2 -By Jeff Fick, Dow Jones Newswires; 55-21-3288-5011; Jeff.Fick@dowjones.com [ 13-12-07 1835GMT ]   \par \par \uc2 1156 1843   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020071213e3cd000zn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
