\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil real eases, stocks cut losses with Wall St.   \b0\par\par\uc2 387 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Oktober 2007\par 22:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Updates to close)   \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO, Oct 24 (Reuters) - Brazil's stock market cut losses to end nearly flat on Wednesday after following international markets down sharply early, while the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  closed weaker due to market volatility.   \par \par \uc2 The Bovespa index <.BVSP> of the Sao Paulo Stock Exchange scrambled late in the day to close down 0.12 percent at 62,624 points after earlier falling to a low of 61,447.   \par \par \uc2 The index has hit a slew of record highs in recent weeks and is up about 40 percent this year.   \par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real <BRBY> weakened in tandem with the Bovespa earlier in the day but closed down 0.61 percent at 1.809 per U.S. dollar. The real has rallied more than 18 percent this year, buoyed by a steady flow of dollars into Latin America's largest economy.   \par \par \uc2 Interest rate futures <0#DIJ:> on the BM&F commodities and futures exchange drifted lower, indicating optimism among investors.   \par \par \uc2 Brazilian markets followed U.S. markets down sharply most of the session after  \uc2 Merrill Lynch & Co Inc\uc2  posted steep quarterly losses and executives warned the worst of the credit crisis may not be over.   \par \par \uc2 But speculation that the U.S. Federal Reserve may cut the discount rate imminently triggered a late-hour rally in New York that reversed most of the day's losses.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  again in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to buy dollars. The bank has been purchasing dollars on a daily basis since Oct. 8, helping to mop up excess dollars in the market and to lift Brazil's foreign reserves to an all-time high of more than $164 billion.   \par \par \uc2 Bovespa's top heavyweight, state oil company  \uc2 Petrobras\uc2  , gained momentum late in the day to close up 4.2 percent at 67.20 reais, far outperforming most of the Brazilian market.   \par \par \uc2 Mining giant CVRD , which will post quarterly earnings on Thursday, fell 2.62 percent to 49.42 reais, however. Analysts on average expect CVRD profit to rise 47 percent from a year ago, according to a Reuters poll. CVRD is the second most heavily weighted stock in the index. (Reporting by Reese Ewing)   \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-BRAZIL (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|E|RBN|U|D|M|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020071024e3ao0027v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
