\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil Mantega: Local Bks Not Highly Exposed To Risk -Estado   \b0\par\par\uc2 683 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 August 2007\par 18:34\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 BRASILIA (Dow Jones)--Brazil's financial system is very solid and has the capacity to weather ongoing turbulence in world markets without severe losses, Brazilian Finance Minister Guido Mantega said Thursday, according to the Estado news agency.   \par \par \uc2 The minister attributed steep declines in Brazilian asset prices over recent sessions to investor panic over weakness in U.S. credit markets and subsequent stop-loss trading. But he said that local banks and other institutions so far haven't suffered great damage from the market shift.   \par \par \uc2 "There is a market exaggeration but then things return to normal," the minister said in an interview with Estado. "Until now, I haven't seen one financial institution here in Brazil show problems." [ 16-08-07 1713GMT ]   \par \par \uc2 Mantega's comments came as Brazil's Bovespa stock index declined for a sixth consecutive session, dropping by more than 8% in Thursday's session to near 45,150 points. The index has racked up losses of more than 22% since July 19, when it closed at a record high of 58,125 points.   \par \par \uc2 Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the real, meanwhile, declined by more than 4% Thursday to BRL2.12 against the dollar, while the country's sovereign debt spread, as measured by the  \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2  Emerging Markets Bond Index Plus, widened by 39 basis points to 239 points over U.S. Treasurys.   \par \par \uc2 The real has weakened by more than 12% against the dollar in the past month amid investor nervousness.   \par \par \uc2 Despite the negative impact of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's depreciation, Mantega said it was too early to ascertain whether the real would remain at its current level, and he sees no need for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been moving in a contrary direction over the past 21 months, buying dollars on the spot market at daily auctions in an effort to build foreign reserves. As of Wednesday, reserves stood at $159.66 billion. [ 16-08-07 1748GMT ]   \par \par \uc2 The minister also said the volatile conditions reinforced the government's recent decision to maintain its target for IPCA consumer price inflation at a conservative level of 4.5% annually. According to recent market surveys by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , IPCA inflation is seen well below that level for the end of 2007, at around 3.8%.   \par \par \uc2 Mantega said that Brazil's low inflation, fiscal stability, and growth potential put it in an advantageous position to receive foreign investment and that this would help steer the country through the current turbulence.   \par \par \uc2 "I don't see how this financial turbulence, even though it has become more serious, can damage Brazil's performance," he said. "Following the turbulence, some countries, as is Brazil's case, will benefit because they have solid fundamentals, offer security, guarantees, and the potential for profits." [ 16-08-07 1821GMT ]   \par \par \uc2 Because of this, the finance minister said Brazil remained in a good position to see an elevation in its sovereign debt ratings to investment grade status.   \par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings and  \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  maintain Brazil's sovereign credit rating at BB+, one notch short of investment grade.   \par \par \uc2 Moody's\uc2  Investor Services rates Brazil Ba2, two notches away from investment grade.  \uc2 Moody's\uc2  officials have said they may meet to review the rating this month, however.   \par \par \uc2 Mantega, meanwhile, lauded recent moves by the U.S. Federal Reserve and the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to reduce turbulence by providing heavy injections of liquidity to the financial system. "They are guaranteeing necessary liquidity at this moment," he said. "The Fed declared that it will provide all the liquidity necessary, and that's a very important stance."   \par \par \uc2 Mantega, however, said he believed that the Fed would have to take additional measures, such as easing interest rates should the situation worsen.   \par \par \uc2 "If there's a worsening of the crisis, the Fed will have to lower rates to counteract high rates in the market, which have occurred because of scarce credit," he said.   \par \par \uc2 -By Gerald Jeffris, Dow Jones Newswires; (5561) 4501-7143; gerald.jeffris@dowjones.com [ 16-08-07 1903GMT ]   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020070816e38g000s5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
