\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2 's Real Closes Stronger Despite Ctrl Bk  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 317 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Mai 2007\par 21:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO (Dow Jones)--The Brazilian real closed stronger against the U.S. dollar Wednesday despite insistent \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 The real closed at BRL2.023 per dollar, stronger against the Monday close of BRL2.035. Brazilian markets were closed Tuesday for the Labor Day holiday.   \par \par \uc2 Traders said the real kept its strength Wednesday, despite \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , because of heavy inflows of U.S. dollars from exporters and foreign investors.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  twice in the market Wednesday, once through a snap auction at which it bought U.S. dollars directly from investors and a second time by auctioning \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap contracts.   \par \par \uc2 The swap contracts normally help to strengthen the dollar by reducing future dollar inflows. The swaps allow investors to switch from dollar-linked paper to bonds indexed to local interest rates. [ 02-05-07 1911GMT ]   \par \par \uc2 Exporters sold dollars heavily into the market Wednesday following another month of huge trade surpluses in April. The April trade surplus was $4.203 billion, up from $3.357 billion in March, the government announced. The government also announced \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  flows for the month of April, showing net inflows of $10.73 billion, up from $6.65 billion in March.   \par \par \uc2 The big net inflows in April came from both exporters and foreign investors attracted by Brazil's booming stock market and by high returns from fixed-income investments. Brazil's base interest rate is currently 12.5%, one of the highest levels in the world.   \par \par \uc2 Credit markets, however, showed expectations for declining interest rates going forward. On the Brazilian Mercantile and Futures Exchange, or BMF, the most heavily traded interest rate futures contract, that of January 2008, closed at 11.56%, down from 11.99% on Monday.   \par \par \uc2 -By Tom Murphy, Dow Jones Newswires; 55-11-3145-1478; brazil@dowjones.com [ 02-05-07 1922GMT ]   \par \par \uc2 1156 1843   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020070502e352000xk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
