\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  Ctrl Bk Nominee Defends  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Mrkt  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 592 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 April 2007\par 16:53\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO (Dow Jones)--The Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should continue its pattern of direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in order to reduce market volatility and build foreign reserves, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor-nominee Mario Gomes Toros said Tuesday.   \par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market whenever there is excessive volatility or lack of liquidity," Toros said. However, he said the main objective of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy should continue to be control over inflation.   \par \par \uc2 Toros also defended the need for continued buildup of foreign reserves. He said, "Brazil has a clear policy of accumulating foreign reserves. Reserves help to redress problems of volatility."   \par \par \uc2 Brazilian foreign reserves as of Monday were $118.5 billion.   \par \par \uc2 Toros made the comments in televised testimony before a Senate committee. The committee will likely vote later Tuesday on Toros' nomination to be the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor in charge of monetary policy. The post is considered the second most important in the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , below that of \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Henrique Meirelles. The committee is widely expected to approve Toros' nomination. [ 24-04-07 1506GMT ]   \par \par \uc2 Questioned about eventual costs associated with maintenance of a high level of foreign reserves, Toros said, "Any costs are more than compensated by the protection a cushion of reserves gives Brazil in times of crisis."   \par \par \uc2 The Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been buying U.S. dollars on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market nearly every day for the last several months, partly to build foreign reserves and partly to slow down the swift appreciation of the Brazilian real against the U.S. dollar.   \par \par \uc2 Typically, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  seeks to "sterilize" the Brazilian reals it emits for such purchases by selling more government bonds on the domestic bond market. Critics have complained that the practice unnecessarily boosts the size of the domestic debt.   \par \par \uc2 The real has gained more than 5% against the U.S. dollar so far in 2007 after gaining 8.8% in 2006. The strength of the real has led to complaints from Brazilian manufacturers that they are losing domestic market share to cheap imports. Exporters have also complained about losing their competitive edge in foreign markets because of the strong real.   \par \par \uc2 Toros said, "We are living through a period of a strong domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , especially in the last two years. This strength is a reflection of our positive economic fundamentals, chiefly our strong foreign trade surplus and strong current account performance."   \par \par \uc2 Brazil rolled up a foreign trade surplus of $46.07 billion in 2006. Earlier Tuesday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  released current account figures showing a 12-month current account surplus through March of $13.396 billion.   \par \par \uc2 Toros noted, however, that "while, on the one hand, the strong real hurts some domestic industries, it also helps control inflation and boost domestic consumption."   \par \par \uc2 He avoided direct comments on monetary policy. Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been criticized by many business leaders and economists for maintenance of high interest rates. The Selic base rate is currently a towering 12.5%.   \par \par \uc2 Toros limited his comments to saying, "The biggest contribution that monetary policy can make is to price stability."   \par \par \uc2 Brazil's official IPCA inflation rate is currently running at a 12-month rate of 2.96%, its lowest in eight years.   \par \par \uc2 Toros added, "Price stability is a necessary condition for sustainable growth. It is not the only condition, but it is one of the necessary conditions."   \par \par \uc2 -By Tom Murphy and Rogerio Jelmayer, Dow Jones Newswires; 55-11-3145-1478; brazil@dowjones.com [ 24-04-07 1525GMT ]   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020070424e34o000ye\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
