\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil's real ends at 9-month high, stocks edge up  \b0\par\par\uc2 428 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Februar 2007\par 14:42\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Updates to close)  \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO, Feb 5 (Reuters) - Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthened to a nine-month high on heavy dollar inflows and stocks edged higher on Monday after Fitch Ratings revised the country's credit outlook to positive, moving it closer to an upgrade.  \par \par \uc2 The real <BRBY> firmed 0.57 percent to 2.094 per U.S. dollar, its strongest close since May 10, 2006, while the Bovespa index <.BVSP> of the Sao Paulo Stock Exchange finished 0.64 percent higher at 45,286.2 points after a late surge. It was the index's fifth straight session of gains.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthened even as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars on the spot \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, as it has done almost daily for months. The \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  have helped boost \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's international reserves to a record high and, indirectly, prevent the real from strengthening further.  \par \par \uc2 With a steady flow of foreign capital pouring into the country from booming exports, some analysts said the real would strengthen even more if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stopped holding daily dollar auctions.  \par \par \uc2 "The only reason the real isn't closer to the 2.0-per-dollar mark is because the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  keeps \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Tarcisio Rodrigues, heading of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at Banco Paulista in Sao Paulo.  \par \par \uc2 The real has also been propped up by bets among investors that the U.S. Federal Reserve will hold off from raising interest rates for some time. High rates in the United States tend to lure cash out of emerging markets like Brazil.  \par \par \uc2 After the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market closed, Fitch Ratings revised the outlook on Brazil's long-term foreign and local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds to positive from stable, citing an improvement in the country's public finances.  \par \par \uc2 It also reaffirmed Brazil's rating at 'BB,' two notches below investment grade, and said the country could be upgraded within the next two years. A higher credit rating would make it easier for Brazil to borrow money abroad and attract a wider pool of investors to the country's capital markets.  \par \par \uc2 Fitch also said on Monday that it had assigned steelmaker Usiminas a 'BBB-' rating, giving it investment grade. That helped drive the company's shares 5.26 percent higher in Sao Paulo to 90.00 reais.  \par \par \uc2 Another steel sector stock posting big gains was Companhia Siderurgica Nacional, or CSN , whose shares rose 3.02 percent to 73.70 reais.  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-BRAZIL (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|E|RBN|M|D|U|RNP|DNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070205e325001db\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
