\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil's stocks rise on CVRD buy;  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  gains  \b0\par\par\uc2 375 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Oktober 2006\par 17:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO, Brazil, Oct 25 (Reuters) - Brazilian stocks rose on Wednesday, helped by mining giant CVRD, while the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gained after the U.S. Federal Reserve issued a statement on interest rates that was less hawkish than feared.  \par \par \uc2 The Bovespa <.BVSP> index of the Sao Paulo Stock Exchange closed up 0.16 percent at 39,562 points after hitting a five-month high in the previous session.  \par \par \uc2 CVRD climbed 1.54 percent to close at 46.70 reais as it purchases Canadian nickel miner Inco for about $13.2 billion.  \par \par \uc2 Arcelor Brasil plunged 7.42 percent to 36.80 reais after global steel giant Arcelor Mittal said it would file a bid for the shares it does not already own in its Brazilian unit, but at a lower price than investors wanted.  \par \par \uc2 Steelmaker CSN fell 1.12 percent to 67.00 reais. The company said on Wednesday it will merge its U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  with Wheeling-Pittsburgh. CSN will contribute its modern steel plant in Terre Haute, Indiana, and $225 million in cash to the new company.  \par \par \uc2 Banco Itau rose 0.68 percent to close at 74 reais even after saying it would take a third-quarter charge of about $818 million for an acquisition it made in May.  \par \par \uc2 In the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, the real <BRBY> strengthened 0.23 percent to close at 2.146 per dollar. It gained despite another \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars on the spot market and build up foreign reserves.  \par \par \uc2 The Fed's statement alleviated worries about interest rate changes for at least a few months. Investment flows to Brazil, Latin America's largest country, usually suffer when U.S. rates rise, but increase when returns in the United States fall.  \par \par \uc2 "Everybody thinks the Fed could start altering rates in January," said Joao Medeiros, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the Pioneer brokerage in Sao Paulo. "If the rest of the economy (inflation and production) stays the same, it could signal lower rates."  \par \par \uc2 Brazil's financial markets have gained in recent weeks, largely unfazed by the presidential run-off vote on Oct. 29.  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-BRAZIL (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|ABN|E|RBN|U|M|D|RNP|DNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020061025e2ap0021s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
