\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil stocks rise led by Net,  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  firms  \b0\par\par\uc2 416 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Oktober 2006\par 16:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO, Brazil, Oct 13 (Reuters) - Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and stocks rallied on Friday, catching up with markets abroad after a public holiday, with shares in pay TV and Internet provider Net soaring on a deal to buy a rival.  \par \par \uc2 The Sao Paulo Stock Exchange's benchmark Bovespa index <.BVSP> was up 1.7 percent at 38,958 points after briefly testing levels slightly above the psychologically important 39,000 point mark. Net was up 4.8 percent at 22.20 reais  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the real <BRBY>, was 0.7 percent stronger at 2.143 as the market, recovering after a slight drop on Wednesday, the last session before the holiday in Brazil Thursday, during which the markets overseas improved.  \par \par \uc2 "The fear that we had on Wednesday about (U.S. Fed's meeting) minutes showed concern about inflation is now mostly gone... Yesterday the Beige Book came in calm, showing that there should be a soft landing and the bourses rose well," said Francisco Carvalho, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at Liquidez brokerage.  \par \par \uc2 Investors fear any Fed interest rate rise could lure investor money toward the world's leading financial markets and away from from riskier emerging markets like \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Traders said \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was likely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  buying dollars again if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  holds on to its early gains.  \par \par \uc2 The Bovespa was also propped up by Thursday's gains abroad despite a small drop in the Dow Industrial Average <.DJI> early on Friday, as it retreated from record highs.  \par \par \uc2 Shares in state-run oil company Petrobras -- the Bovespa's most-traded paper -- rose 2.2 percent to 41.6 reais on a rebound in world oil prices.  \par \par \uc2 Net rallied over 4 percent after Thursday's announcement of a deal to buy a minority stake in local rival Vivax with future acquisition of control. Under the deal, each Vivax share will be swapped for 0.5678 preferred share in Net. Vivax shares fell 2 percent to 37.2 reais on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 UBS analysts said in a report they believed Vivax was a good partnership for Net, as their systems would complement each other and ultimately boost Net's earnings.  \par \par \uc2 "Net is buying Vivax with a discount of 8.2 percent compared to our adjusted target price. And that before you include any synergies, which can be relevant," UBS analysts said in a report.  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-BRAZIL|LANGEN|ABN|E|RBN|U|D|M|RNP|DNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020061013e2ad001i3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw202\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3030\pichgoal600
